#concept-box("How to use this appendix")[
  Cover the model box with a sheet of paper, write your answer, and only then slide the paper down. The model answers carry the mark allocation in brackets, so you can see exactly where each mark is earned — and in inorganic chemistry most of the marks are won by naming the *condition* or the *observation*, not by the arithmetic.
]

== I.1 Metals and the Reactivity Series

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 1.* A student adds pieces of magnesium, zinc and copper to separate test tubes of dilute hydrochloric acid. \
  #v(3pt)
  State and explain the order in which the three metals react. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the reaction of zinc with the acid. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why copper does not react, using the idea of electron transfer. (2 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Magnesium reacts fastest, zinc steadily, copper not at all (1). The reactivity series places the metals in the order Mg > Zn > Cu (1), and the rate of reaction follows that order because the more reactive metal loses its electrons more readily (1).
    \
    *(b)* $"Zn" + "2HCl" -> "ZnCl"_2 + "H"_2$ (2 — one mark for the correct products, one for balancing).
    \
    *(c)* Copper is below hydrogen in the reactivity series, so its atoms hold their electrons more strongly than hydrogen atoms do (1). There is no energetic advantage in copper giving electrons to H⁺, so the redox reaction cannot go (1).
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 2.* Iron is extracted in a blast furnace. \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the ore used and give its formula. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the reducing agent and write the equation for its formation from coke. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain the purpose of the limestone in the furnace. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  State one reason why the iron from a blast furnace is not used as it is. (2 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Haematite, Fe₂O₃ (2 — one for the name, one for the formula). Magnetite Fe₃O₄ is equally acceptable.
    \
    *(b)* Carbon monoxide (1). It is formed by the burning of coke at the tuyères, $"C" + "O"_2 -> "CO"_2$, followed by $"CO"_2 + "C" -> "2CO"$ (2).
    \
    *(c)* The limestone decomposes to calcium oxide, which reacts with the silica impurity to form calcium silicate slag (1). The slag floats on the molten iron and can be run off, so the impurity is removed (1).
    \
    *(d)* It contains about 4 % carbon, which makes it hard and brittle (1), so it must be converted to steel before it can be used for structural work (1).
  ]

  == I.2 Groups 1 and 2
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 3.* A student drops a small piece of sodium into a trough of water and then repeats the experiment with potassium. \
  #v(3pt)
  State two observations that show potassium is more reactive than sodium. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the reaction of potassium with water. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain the trend in reactivity down Group 1 in terms of atomic structure. (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* The potassium melts into a ball and moves faster over the surface (1); the hydrogen it produces ignites with a lilac flame, which sodium does not normally do (1).
    \
    *(b)* $"2K" + "2H"_2 "O" -> "2KOH" + "H"_2$ (2 — one for the products, one for balancing).
    \
    *(c)* Down the group the atoms get larger and gain an extra shell (1); the outer electron is further from the nucleus and is shielded by the inner shells (1); so it is lost more easily, and the metal is more reactive (1).
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 4.* Calcium carbonate is heated strongly in a lime kiln. \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the decomposition. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  State what is observed when water is added to the product. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain how the product is used to remove temporary hardness, giving the equation. (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* $"CaCO"_3 -> "CaO" + "CO"_2$ (2 — one for the products, one for balancing).
    \
    *(b)* The white solid crumbles and hisses (1), and the mixture becomes hot, giving off steam (1). Calcium hydroxide is formed.
    \
    *(c)* Calcium hydroxide reacts with the dissolved calcium hydrogencarbonate to precipitate calcium carbonate (1), which settles out and is filtered off (1). $"Ca(HCO"_3")"_2 + "Ca(OH)"_2 -> "2CaCO"_3 + "2H"_2 "O"$ (1). The hardness is removed because the hydrogencarbonate, which caused it, is gone.
  ]

  == I.3 Aluminium, Iron and Steel
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 5.* Aluminium is extracted by the electrolysis of alumina dissolved in molten cryolite. \
  #v(3pt)
  State why the alumina is dissolved in cryolite rather than electrolysed on its own. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the reaction at each electrode. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why the carbon anodes have to be replaced at intervals. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Calculate the mass of aluminium that could be obtained from 5.10 kg of pure alumina. (A_r: Al = 27, O = 16) (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Alumina melts at over 2000 °C, far above the working temperature of the cell (1); cryolite dissolves it and allows electrolysis at about 950 °C, which saves a great deal of energy (1).
    \
    *(b)* At the cathode, $"Al"^(3+) + "3e"^- -> "Al"$ (1). At the anode, $"2O"^(2-) -> "O"_2 + "4e"^-$ (2 — one for the product, one for the electrons).
    \
    *(c)* The oxygen produced at the anode reacts with the hot carbon, forming carbon dioxide (1), so the anode is gradually burnt away and must be renewed (1).
    \
    *(d)* $n("Al"_2 "O"_3) = 5100 div 102 = 50.0$ mol (1). Each mole of alumina contains two moles of aluminium, so $n("Al") = 100$ mol (1). $m = 100 times 27 = 2700$ g $= 2.70$ kg (1).
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 6.* Steel is made from the iron produced in a blast furnace. \
  #v(3pt)
  State the main impurity in pig iron and the element used to remove it. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Describe how stainless steel differs from mild steel in composition and in property. (4 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain, with an equation, how zinc protects iron from rusting even when the coating is scratched. (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Carbon (1), removed by blowing oxygen onto the molten iron (1).
    \
    *(b)* Stainless steel contains chromium, and usually nickel, in addition to iron and carbon (1), while mild steel has only a low percentage of carbon (1). The chromium forms a thin, adherent oxide layer that protects the metal beneath (1), so stainless steel resists corrosion where mild steel would rust (1).
    \
    *(c)* Zinc is above iron in the reactivity series, so it loses electrons in preference to iron (1). $"Zn" -> "Zn"^(2+) + "2e"^-$ (1). The zinc corrodes instead of the iron, which is sacrificial protection, and the iron remains intact even where the coating is broken (1).
  ]

  == I.4 Hydrogen, Oxygen and Nitrogen
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 7.* Ammonia is manufactured by the Haber process. \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation and state the conditions used. (4 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why the yield at equilibrium is greater at high pressure. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why the industrial temperature is higher than the temperature that would give the greatest yield. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  State two uses of ammonia. (2 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* $"N"_2 + "3H"_2 <=> "2NH"_3$ (2). Iron catalyst, 450 °C, 200 atm (2 — one mark for the catalyst, one for the temperature and pressure).
    \
    *(b)* There are four moles of gas on the left and two on the right, so the forward reaction reduces the volume (1). By Le Chatelier's principle, raising the pressure shifts the equilibrium to the side with fewer gas molecules, that is, to the ammonia (1).
    \
    *(c)* The forward reaction is exothermic, so a low temperature would give a higher equilibrium yield (1); but the rate would be far too slow to be economic, so a compromise temperature is used with a catalyst to restore the rate (1).
    \
    *(d)* Any two of: making fertilisers, making nitric acid by the Ostwald process, as a refrigerant, in cleaning agents (2).
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 8.* A gas is evolved when a solution of a nitrate salt is warmed with iron(II) sulphate and concentrated sulphuric acid is poured down the side of the tube. \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the test being carried out and the observation that confirms a nitrate. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why dilute nitric acid does not give hydrogen with copper, although dilute hydrochloric acid does with zinc. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the reaction of copper with concentrated nitric acid and state the colour of the gas. (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* The brown ring test (1). A brown ring forms at the junction of the two layers (1), which shows that a nitrate is present (1).
    \
    *(b)* Nitric acid is an oxidising acid (1): the nitrate ion is reduced in preference to the hydrogen ion (1), so nitrogen oxides are evolved rather than hydrogen. Hydrochloric acid is not an oxidising acid, so zinc reduces H⁺ to hydrogen (1).
    \
    *(c)* $"Cu" + "4HNO"_3 -> "Cu(NO"_3")"_2 + "2NO"_2 + "2H"_2 "O"$ (2). The gas is brown (1).
  ]

  == I.5 Sulphur and the Halogens
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 9.* Sulphuric acid is made by the Contact process. \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the raw materials and the catalyst. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the key equilibrium and state the conditions. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why sulphur(VI) oxide is absorbed in concentrated sulphuric acid rather than in water. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Calculate the mass of sulphur needed to make 1.00 tonne of sulphuric acid, assuming complete conversion. (A_r: S = 32, H = 1, O = 16) (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Sulphur (or a sulphide ore), air and water (2); vanadium(V) oxide, V₂O₅ (1).
    \
    *(b)* $"2SO"_2 + "O"_2 <=> "2SO"_3$ (1), over V₂O₅ at about 450 °C and 2 atm (2).
    \
    *(c)* Water reacts with sulphur(VI) oxide to give a fine mist of sulphuric acid droplets (1), which is corrosive and hard to condense; concentrated acid absorbs the gas smoothly and the oleum formed is diluted afterwards (1).
    \
    *(d)* $n("H"_2 "SO"_4) = 1.00 times 10^6 div 98 = 1.02 times 10^4$ mol (1). Each mole of acid contains one mole of sulphur, so the same number of moles of sulphur is needed (1). $m = 1.02 times 10^4 times 32 = 3.27 times 10^5$ g $= 0.327$ t (1).
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 10.* Chlorine is bubbled through cold dilute sodium hydroxide solution. \
  #v(3pt)
  State what is observed and name the two salts formed. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation and explain why the reaction is a disproportionation. (4 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  State one large-scale use of the product mixture. (1 mark) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain why chlorine bleaches damp litmus paper but dry litmus paper is unaffected. (3 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* The yellow-green colour of the chlorine fades (1); sodium chloride and sodium chlorate(I) are formed (2).
    \
    *(b)* $"Cl"_2 + "2NaOH" -> "NaCl" + "NaClO" + "H"_2 "O"$ (2). The oxidation state of chlorine is 0 in the element, −1 in the chloride and +1 in the chlorate(I) (1), so the same element is both oxidised and reduced (1).
    \
    *(c)* It is the active ingredient of household bleach, and the solution is also used as a disinfectant (1).
    \
    *(d)* Chlorine must react with water to form hypochlorous acid, which is the bleaching agent (1). $"Cl"_2 + "H"_2 "O" <=> "HCl" + "HOCl"$ (1). Dry paper has no water for the reaction, so no hypochlorous acid is formed and no bleaching occurs (1).
  ]

  == I.6 Chemical Industry and the Environment
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 11.* Cement is manufactured in a rotary kiln. \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the two main raw materials and state the temperature of the kiln. (3 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Write the equation for the reaction that takes place in the kiln. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  State the purpose of the gypsum added to the clinker. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Explain one environmental problem caused by cement manufacture. (2 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Limestone and clay (2); about 1450 °C (1).
    \
    *(b)* $"CaCO"_3 -> "CaO" + "CO"_2$ (2).
    \
    *(c)* The gypsum controls the rate at which the cement sets (2). Without it the cement would set too quickly to be worked.
    \
    *(d)* The kiln releases a large quantity of carbon dioxide, both from the burning fuel and from the decomposition of the limestone itself (2), which contributes to the greenhouse effect.
  ]
]

#block(breakable: false)[
  *Question 12.* A sample of well water lathers only with difficulty and gives a white precipitate with soap. \
  #v(3pt)
  Name the two ions responsible for the hardness. (2 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Describe a test to distinguish temporary from permanent hardness, and state the result you would expect for each. (4 marks) \
  #v(3pt)
  Describe how the water could be softened on a large scale, and explain the principle. (4 marks)

  #block(breakable: false, width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
    *(a)* Calcium and magnesium ions (2).
    \
    *(b)* Boil equal volumes of the water in two test tubes and add soap to each (1). If a lather forms easily after boiling, the hardness was temporary (1). If it still does not lather, the hardness is permanent (1), because boiling decomposes only the hydrogencarbonates (1).
    \
    *(c)* Pass the water through an ion-exchange resin (1), which swaps the calcium and magnesium ions for sodium ions (1). Sodium salts do not form a scum with soap (1), so the water lathers freely; the resin can be regenerated with brine (1).
  ]
]

#concept-box("What the twelve questions have in common")[
  Every model answer above is built the same way: *name the species*, write the equation, then state the condition or the observation that the mark is attached to. The arithmetic is never the hard part — the hard part is remembering that copper needs the concentrated acid, that ammonia is dried with calcium oxide, that sulphur(VI) oxide is absorbed in acid and not in water. Answer the twelve questions twice, a fortnight apart, and the explanations you write in the second sitting will be shorter and more accurate; that shortening is the exam skill.
]
